<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>
<section xml:id="infrastructure-introduction-github-intro">
  <title>GitHub: Getting Started</title>
<introduction>
  <p>To get started with GitHub you should know about creating an account,
    the basics of markdown, and how to view repository and file history.
  </p>
  </introduction>
  <subsection xml:id="infrastructure-introduction-github-intro-creating-account">
    <title>Creating an Account</title>
    <p>
        You may already have an account. If so, then if you have a .edu account, and 
        it's not linked to your GitHub account, then you should link it to your GitHub account, 
        and then you can go to the next section.
    </p>
    <p>
        If you don't already have an account, then there a few things
        that you may want to consider. First, is your <term>username</term>. Your
        username is important because it is how most of your repositories 
        are located. Select a username that you think you'll be happy with
        even after a few years. Remember, if you end up sharing code with a 
        potential employer, then you may want your username to be work appropriate.
    </p>
    <p>
        Another thing to consider is password management. GitHub allows you to use
        other OAuth systems, such a Google and Apple accounts, to sign up with GitHub.
        So you don't have to store a new password, but you can create a new password.
        Since most modern operating systems now provide automatic password creation and local 
        storage of passwords in an encrypted area.
        In addition, GitHub wants you to use two-factor identification, and it will guide
        you through your options for that.
    </p>
    <p>
        For two-factor identification there are many options. It's good
        to have more than one such as a passkey and the GitHub mobile app.
    </p>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="infrastructure-introduction-github-intro-markdown">
    <title>Using Markdown</title>
    <p>
        GitHub uses Markdown as it's default web rendering language. Markdown is a plain text formatting
        language like html. In fact Markdown has html as an option for formatting parts of a document that
        Markdown doesn't have a formatter for. Any file with <c>.md</c> as a
        suffix will be processed as a Markdown file, and the html version of the file will be shown 
        instead of the raw text. 
    </p>
    <p> 
        To learn about markdown you can use <url href="https://docs.github.com/en/get-started/writing-on-github/getting-started-with-writing-and-formatting-on-github">GitHub' Markdown documentation</url>.

    </p>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="infrastructure-introduction-github-intro-file-history">
<title> Repository and File History</title>
<p>
    One of the great things about version control is that you don't need to 
    rename files with version histories in order to keep track of the changes 
    you've made. Instead, you need to add and commit your changes that you want
    to remember. The key is that the filename stays the same, so most of the time
    you'll have the latest version of the file active in your repository. 
</p>
<p>
    To see other versions you can browse the history logs in GitHub. 
    Below are some examples of looking at repository history.
    <ul>
    <li><url href="https://github.com/godotengine/godot/commits/master/">This links to an example history log of Godot</url>
    </li><li><url href="https://github.com/godotengine/godot/commits/master/README.md">This links to an example
        history of just the README.md file of Godot</url>
        </li><li><url href="https://github.com/godotengine/godot/commit/925fbbc5c4cb34ca4377b3231b2dc1b8d3a7d187#diff-b335630551682c19a781afebcf4d07bf978fb1f8ac04c6bf87428ed5106870f5">This shows the diff between the 
            latest commit of the README.md file and it's previous commit</url>
</li></ul>
        </p>
        <p>If you remember the commit message that you used for the version 
            that you want, then you can look at the history log and then click
            that commit message and it will bring you to all of the changes for 
            that commit, then there is a "Browse Files" button near the upper right
            that will bring you to a web view of the files at the time of the commit.
            Then you can browse to the file that you want to look at, and either copy
            specific text from it or download the whole file to start again from it.
            Then after making the changes, you can add and commit the file locally and 
            then push the change so that everyone will have access to the corrected version.
        </p>
  </subsection>
 
</section>
